{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Foundations of Interactive Multimedia Learning","title":"Worked Examples and Fading","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting worked examples and fading to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_foundations_of_interactive_multimedia_learning","ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"04","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/01/03"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_calibration"]},"content":"# Worked Examples and Fading\n\n![Worked Examples and Fading textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/01-04-worked-examples-and-fading.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThere is a counterintuitive result at the center of this section: for novices, studying a fully worked solution produces more learning than solving an equivalent problem, even though solving feels like more learning is happening. This is the [worked-example effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worked-example_effect), and it follows directly from [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). A novice solving an unguided problem spends working memory on search and trial, which is extraneous to building a schema; a novice studying a worked example spends that capacity on the solution structure itself. The design move this section teaches, fading, is how you move a learner from studied examples to independent performance without ever dropping them into the unguided-search trap. Your storyboard sequence is where that fade is engineered, frame by frame.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Explain the [worked-example effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worked-example_effect) in terms of search load versus schema-building load.\n- Engineer a fading sequence (worked, completion, open) matched to the learner's growing schema.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA module asks novices to \"write a measurable learning objective\" across four open prompts. Learners stare, guess, and produce flailing attempts, because they have no schema for the parts of an objective. The instinct is to add more practice problems. But practice volume is not the constraint; the learners are searching a space they cannot yet navigate. The real decision is where to place a model, and how to withdraw it, so that effort lands on building the schema rather than on blind search.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe [worked-example effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worked-example_effect) rests on the distinction between two ways a novice can spend working memory. **Problem-solving search** (means-ends analysis: where am I, where is the goal, what reduces the gap?) consumes capacity without leaving a schema behind. **Studying a worked solution** lets the learner attend to the solution's structure and build the schema directly. For novices, search is largely extraneous load, so worked examples win.\n\nBut the advantage is conditional. The **[expertise-reversal effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expertise_reversal_effect)** says that once a learner has a schema, studying a worked example becomes redundant with what they already know, and now problem-solving is the better use of capacity. So the right design is not \"always show examples\" or \"always make them practice.\" It is a **fading sequence** that tracks the learner's growth:\n\n| Stage | Format | What the learner does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Worked example | Studies and self-explains a complete solution |\n| 2 | Completion problem | Finishes a partly worked solution (some steps faded) |\n| 3 | Completion problem | Finishes more (more steps faded) |\n| 4 | Open problem | Solves unaided |\n\nTwo design refinements matter. **Backward fading** removes the last step first (the step closest to the goal, often the easiest to attempt), so the learner always works from a supported position. And a **self-explanation prompt** on the worked stages converts passive viewing into the germane processing that actually builds the schema.\n\nOrder the four support formats from most to least scaffolding, the sequence a true novice should travel as the schema forms.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Fully worked example: study and self-explain a complete solution\", \"Completion problem with two of four parts faded in\", \"Completion problem with three of four parts faded in\", \"Open problem: solve the whole task unaided\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA thin claim hides the instructional move:\n\n> I made the objective-writing sequence clearer.\n\nA fading-grounded claim names the stages and the schema target:\n\n> I replaced four open prompts with a four-stage fade: a fully worked objective with its four parts labeled, then two completion problems that withdraw parts one at a time, then an open problem. Support is removed as the schema forms, so effort lands on the parts of an objective rather than on guessing.\n\nThe improvement is that support is transferred one part at a time, so the learner moves from studying a model to independent performance rather than being dropped into open problems with no schema.\n\n### Annotated Before/After: A Faded Sequence\n\nHere is a storyboard sequence for teaching novices to write a measurable learning objective, revised to apply the [worked-example effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worked-example_effect) with fading.\n\n**Before.** Frames 1 through 4 each pose an open task: \"Write a measurable objective for this scenario.\" No model is shown. Reader-action note: \"submit your objective.\"\n\nThe diagnosis: novices have no schema for the parts of an objective (audience, behavior, condition, degree), so four unguided attempts mostly produce flailing and high extraneous effort.\n\n**After (four faded frames).**\n\n1. Frame 1, fully worked example: a complete objective is shown with each of its four parts labeled and a one-line rationale for each. Reader-action: study and self-explain.\n2. Frame 2, completion problem: the audience and behavior are given; the learner supplies the condition and degree. (Two of four parts faded in.)\n3. Frame 3, completion problem: only the behavior verb is given; the learner supplies the other three parts. (Three of four faded in.)\n4. Frame 4, open problem: the learner writes a full objective from the scenario unaided.\n\n**Rejected suggestion.** An AI assistant proposed making all four frames open problems \"so learners get more practice.\" Rejected: for these novices it removes the modeled schema and recreates the original failure. Practice volume is not the constraint; guided schema-building is.\n\nThe improvement is that support is transferred one part at a time, so the learner moves from studying a model to independent performance rather than being dropped into open problems with no schema.\n\nReason through the design of this fade one decision at a time, predicting before each reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Engineering the objective-writing fade\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Frame 1 for true novices who have no schema for the parts of an objective. What should it be?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"An open problem so they get practice immediately\",\"A fully worked objective with its four parts labeled and a one-line rationale\",\"A multiple-choice quiz on objective parts\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"For novices with no schema, the first frame is a complete, self-explained model. An open problem here is a search trap, because the learner must invent the category while producing one.\"},{\"prompt\":\"What single addition turns the worked frame from passive viewing into schema-building?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A countdown timer\",\"A self-explanation prompt asking why each part is correct\",\"A brighter color scheme\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The self-explanation prompt provokes the germane processing that builds the schema; without it a worked example is just a video to watch.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Each completion frame then withdraws one part at a time (two parts faded, then three) so the learner is never in unsupported search, and only the final frame is open. The all-open suggestion is rejected because it recreates the original search trap: practice volume was never the constraint.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nMatch the support level to the schema, and fade only as the schema grows:\n\n1. **Start worked for true novices.** If the learner has no schema, the first frame is a complete, self-explained model, not a problem.\n2. **Fade one element at a time.** Each completion stage withdraws a single part so the learner is never in unsupported search.\n3. **Reach open only when the schema can carry it.** The final unaided frame is the assessment of the fade, not its starting point.\n\nIf you cannot say which element each stage withdraws and why now, the sequence is a list of tasks, not a fade.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"more practice is more learning,\" which for novices reintroduces the search load the worked example removed. The second is treating a worked example as a video to watch passively; without a self-explanation prompt, the germane processing never happens and the example teaches little.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A cohort already writes objectives competently but inconsistently applies the degree criterion. You give them the full four-stage fade starting with a worked example. Is this the right design?\" explanation=\"By the [expertise-reversal effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expertise_reversal_effect), a fully worked example is redundant for learners who already hold the schema, so it wastes their time and teaches less than a targeted problem. The right move is to drop the early worked and completion frames and give open problems that stress the weak element (degree).\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, the full fade is always the safest sequence\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, for learners with a schema the early worked frames are redundant; start higher with targeted open problems\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, because more scaffolding is always better\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n The third is ignoring reversal and keeping fully worked examples for learners who have already formed the schema, where the example is now redundant and a problem would teach more.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace why the four-open-prompt version failed and why the fade fixes it. A novice at prompt 1 must invent the very category \"parts of an objective\" while also producing one, that is search load piled on intrinsic load, and capacity saturates. The fade reorders the spend: frame 1 hands the learner the four-part schema as a studied model, so capacity goes to understanding structure, not searching. By frame 2 the learner has the schema and can supply two parts; by frame 3, three; by frame 4 the schema carries an unaided attempt. The withdrawal is gradual and element-wise, so the learner is always working from support rather than from scratch. The rejected \"all open\" suggestion is exactly the original failure, which is why it is rejected on principle, not preference.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every practice sequence and ask whether the learner has the schema the first task assumes. A strong reader can name, for each frame, the support level, the element being withdrawn, and the schema state that justifies the withdrawal. Keep a two-column note: frame on the left, support level and faded element on the right.\n\nA strong reader of worked examples and fading can answer four questions:\n\n1. Does the learner have a schema, or is the first frame a search trap?\n2. Which element does each stage withdraw, and why now?\n3. Where is the self-explanation prompt that makes a worked example generative?\n4. At what expertise level should the worked stages be dropped entirely?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe sequence is strong when each frame states its support level (worked, completion, open), the element it withdraws, and the schema condition that licenses the withdrawal, with a self-explanation prompt on the worked stages. \"Four practice frames\" is weak; \"worked then two completion frames withdrawing one part each then open, with self-explanation on frame 1\" is strong. If a frame's support level is unstated, mark the gap and ask BigAL to compare a novice fade with an expert fade for the same skill rather than to define worked examples.\n\nA useful final entry would read: \"I built a four-stage fade that withdraws one objective part per frame and added self-explanation to frame 1. I rejected the all-open version because it recreates the search trap for novices.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the sequence to a graduate cohort who already writes objectives competently but inconsistently applies the degree criterion. A full four-stage fade would bore them and, by reversal, teach less than targeted problems. The right transfer is to drop frames 1 and 2 entirely and give two open problems that stress the weak element (degree), with feedback. The fading principle is intact; the starting support level moves up because the schema is already partly built. A designer who engineered the fade by schema state can re-anchor it; one who just sequenced four tasks cannot.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/01-04-worked-examples-and-fading-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"01.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 01.04. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure each frame states its support level, the element it withdraws, the schema condition that licenses the withdrawal, and a self-explanation prompt on the worked stages, plus any suggestion you rejected and why.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the fading sequence to the revision evidence each frame records and the bounded role an AI assistant plays when it proposes changing the support level.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading","stem":"A storyboard for novices gives ten open practice problems and zero worked examples. Citing the worked-example effect, what redesign is best justified?","options":["Keep all ten as open problems; novices learn best by struggling unaided.","Replace the first several problems with fully worked examples studied step by step, then move toward open problems.","Add more open problems so learners get more practice.","Give the answers only at the end so learners can check themselves."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The worked-example effect: for novices, studying worked examples produces better learning and less wasted effort than unguided problem solving, because it shows the solution schema directly.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_we_practice_always_beats_examples","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading","stem":"A designer plans a sequence of fully worked example, then completion problem, then open problem. What is the design purpose of the middle completion problem?","options":["To add length so the lesson feels more substantial.","It is a fading step: the learner completes the remaining steps of a partly worked solution, bridging from studying to independent solving.","To test the learner before any instruction is given.","To repeat the worked example exactly so learners memorize it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A completion problem fades support by giving a partial solution and asking the learner to finish it, scaffolding the transition from worked example to independent problem.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_we_fading_is_removing_examples","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading","stem":"After several modules, an advanced cohort still receives only fully worked examples and reports boredom and weaker gains. Which effect explains this, and what should the designer do?","options":["The novelty effect; add more worked examples.","The expertise-reversal effect; fade the worked support and shift these now-experienced learners toward open problem solving.","The redundancy effect; narrate the worked examples aloud.","Nothing; worked examples are best for all learners at all times."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Expertise reversal: as learners gain schemas, continued full worked support becomes redundant and can depress learning. The designer should fade support toward independent solving.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_we_examples_always_best","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_worked_examples_and_fading"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_worked_examples_and_fading_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"A worked example for a multi-step calculation shows only the final answer with the note 'steps omitted for brevity.' Why does this undercut the worked-example effect?","options":["Because worked examples should never show the final answer.","Because the benefit comes from studying the intermediate solution steps and their rationale; hiding them leaves the learner with no schema to study.","Because brevity always improves learning.","Because learners should compute the final answer themselves first."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The learning value of a worked example lies in the visible, explained intermediate steps. Omitting them turns it back into an answer key, removing the modeled schema.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_4_we_answer_is_the_example","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"1f0ea0ae9a1aa251888ddf695643590e85c56c030ac2f2c1c84dc6a70066b999","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}